{"id": 98, "ts": 1782863298.4078085, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I appreciate Agent\u202f1\u2019s candid acknowledgment of the evidence gaps for Candidate\u202fA. While A\u2019s technical edge\u2014anticipating dangerous situations, excellent spatial vision, and strong leadership\u2014remains compelling, the lack of any concrete observations on stress resilience, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, and especially feedback orientation (notably \u201csometimes not good at taking criticism\u201d) is a serious concern for a long\u2011distance, safety\u2011critical role.\nThe new input from Agent\u202f2 confirms that we also have no direct observations for Candidate\u202fD on crew cooperation, professional communication, or feedback orientation, and only a minimal note that D \u201cresponds to unexpected events adequately\u201d and \u201ccan concentrate very well.\u201d These limited data points do not offset D\u2019s reported arrogance and know\u2011it\u2011all attitude, which could further impair teamwork and communication.\nGiven that both A and D suffer from missing evidence on key interpersonal criteria, we should prioritize the candidate for whom we have the strongest overall profile across the required dimensions. Candidate\u202fB and Candidate\u202fC each have clear strengths (B\u2019s stress handling and conscientiousness; C\u2019s 100\u202f% reliability and calmness in crisis) but also notable weaknesses in communication and cooperation. However, unlike A, B and C have documented reliability and stress resilience, and we have no indication they struggle with feedback as A does.\nTherefore, I remain provisionally voting for **Candidate\u202fA**, but only if we can obtain at least one concrete observation confirming A\u2019s ability to work cooperatively in a crew, communicate professionally, and accept feedback. Without that, the balance of evidence may shift toward a more reliably cooperative candidate."}
